CLWA claims: engine = universal behavioral-physics laws + empty registries; modules fill them; engine NEVER imports modules, ZERO module-specific strings (INV-14). The promise: a NEW game = a new data MODULE, no engine changes. We are EMPIRICALLY testing that promise: for a game of a COMPLETELY different focus than crime-detective, would you truly need only a module, or would you ALSO keep changing the engine? Be concrete and skeptical — trace actual registries/contracts, don't take the claim on faith. Reference: engine registries in packages/engine/src/event-registry.ts; what a module registers in packages/modules/src/crime-justice/index.ts (and social/index.ts); engine subsystems in packages/engine/src/{al,volition,cg-engine,cg-types,beliefs,il,goals,world,agent-types,world-types}.ts.

Synthesize the PRECISE answer to: "How self-sufficient is the engine — for a different-focus game, only a module, or also engine changes?" Inputs (JSON):

STATIC AUDIT (clwa-auditor):
{
  "moduleAgnosticVerdict": "ALMOST MODULE-AGNOSTIC, BUT NOT FULLY. The engine is approximately 95% module-agnostic. 40+ registries cover the extension surface comprehensively — event types, source flags, step handlers, discovery handlers, day-advance handlers, tick phase handlers, consequence post-handlers, affordances, target finders, requirement checkers, and more are all cleanly delegated. HOWEVER, the engine contains several concrete leaks of crime-justice module vocabulary that break INV-14 in practice. The critical test: \"If I add an Economy module with FRAUD/SMUGGLING — does the engine handle it without code changes?\" MOSTLY YES for events/affordances/CG/goals. But: (1) engine hardcodes 'rumor' as a belief source string in world.ts belief-transfer code — an Economy module with different gossip mechanics would need to reuse this exact string or the engine ignores its belief transfers; (2) engine pre-seeds default BELIEF_SOURCE_LABELS and SOURCE_TRANSFER_WEIGHTS for crime-specific source strings like 'body_discovery', 'deduction', 'evidence' — an Economy module would have to override these or accept irrelevant defaults; (3) engine agent.ts builds prompts with crime-specific prose (\"You are wanted for a crime\", \"the guard coming for you\", \"ACTIVE DUTY: investigating the [crime] of [victim]\") that only make sense in a crime context; (4) secret-directives.ts in engine contains inline crime-specific behavioral prose; (5) concepts like CrimeScene, processKill, markCrimeScene are engine-level primitives with crime-specific naming and semantics. The ARCHITECTURE is sound — registries genuinely work, the separation pattern is proven. But the implementation has accumulated crime-justice module knowledge in the engine layer.",
  "leaks": [
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/world.ts:1181,1194,1309",
      "issue": "Hardcoded 'rumor' belief source string in engine. 'rumor' is NOT in BELIEF_SOURCE constants — it's a crime-justice module source registered via registerSourceFlags/registerSourceDecayRates. Engine should use a BELIEF_SOURCE.GOSSIP constant or a registry lookup for conversation-transferred beliefs, not a hardcoded module string.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/event-registry.ts:312-318",
      "issue": "BELIEF_SOURCE_LABELS pre-seeded with crime-specific defaults ('body_discovery' -> 'found the body', 'deduction' -> 'deduced from clues'). These are crime-justice concepts baked into engine defaults. Should start empty, modules register all labels. Modules already call registerBeliefSourceLabels() with their own — these defaults are redundant AND leak module knowledge.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/event-registry.ts:332-338",
      "issue": "BELIEF_SOURCE constants include 'body_discovery' and 'deduction' — crime-justice-specific source strings promoted to engine constants. An Economy module has no concept of body discovery or crime deduction. TESTIMONY and OBSERVATION are arguably universal; BODY_DISCOVERY, EVIDENCE, DEDUCTION are module vocabulary.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/event-registry.ts:347-353",
      "issue": "SOURCE_TRANSFER_WEIGHTS pre-seeded with crime-specific defaults ('body_discovery': 0.7, 'deduction': 0.6). Same issue as BELIEF_SOURCE_LABELS — module knowledge in engine defaults. Modules already override via registerSourceTransferWeights().",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/agent.ts:371-406",
      "issue": "buildRoleDirective() contains crime-specific prompt prose hardcoded in engine: 'ACTIVE DUTY: You are investigating the {crime} of {victim}. Suspect: {suspect}.' and 'You are wanted for a crime. {fear about the guard}.' This is crime-justice behavioral logic in the engine. An Economy module's investigator would get 'ACTIVE DUTY: investigating the fraud of victim' — accidentally appropriate — but the 'guard coming for you' line and 'wanted for a crime' text are crime-specific. Should be a registry of role-directive builders that modules fill.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/secret-directives.ts:20-36,38-60,63-89",
      "issue": "Engine's buildSecretDirectives() contains inline crime-specific behavioral prose: 'You committed {crime} against {name}. Nobody must find out.' and 'deny any involvement', 'become defensive', 'redirect suspicion'. The covering-up section: 'You saw {name} commit {crime}, but you are protecting them'. The witness section: 'You want justice'. While it uses registry flags (isTransferable, isConcealment, isFirsthand) for detection, the RESPONSE TEXT is crime-specific behavioral guidance. An Economy module NPC who committed fraud would get 'deny involvement, redirect suspicion' — which happens to work — but the framing assumes adversarial criminal concealment, not all transferable events.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/world-types.ts:114-150, crime-scene.ts, world.ts:1485-1519",
      "issue": "CrimeScene, createCrimeScene(), markCrimeScene(), ExaminationPoint — these are engine-level primitives with 'crime' in their names and crime-specific semantics (perpetratorId, victimId, personsPresent). While they're template-driven via registries, the concept of 'crime scene' is itself module vocabulary. An Economy module has no 'crime scenes'. These should be renamed to generic 'EventScene' or 'IncidentScene' concepts — or moved to modules entirely.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/world.ts:1529-1568",
      "issue": "processKill() is an engine-level method that handles a specific crime-justice event (killing). The engine provides a method specifically for one module's event type. An Economy module doesn't kill NPCs. This should be a generic processAgentDeath() or moved to modules as a step handler. Currently it accesses engine internals (eventStore.append, bodyDiscoveries, crimeScenes) so it can't trivially move, but the function name and framing is crime-specific.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/al.ts:33-48 (type definition comments)",
      "issue": "AffordanceRequirements interface has JSDoc comments referencing 'NPC_KILLED/GOODS_STOLEN', 'WANTED_STATUS', 'detained', 'criminal' — module-specific event types in type definition comments. While comments don't affect runtime, they couple documentation to one module's vocabulary. The requirement NAMES themselves (isCriminal, isWanted, hasWitnessBelief, deductionBelowThreshold, hasCrimeBelief, notPerpetrator) are crime-specific vocabulary in the engine type system.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/al.ts:14-15,19-20,27-30",
      "issue": "AffordanceRequirements type has fields named 'noGuardNearby', 'notGuard', 'isGuard', 'hasCrimeBelief', 'isCriminal', 'isWanted', 'hasWitnessBelief', 'notPerpetrator', 'deductionBelowThreshold'. While these are checked via REQUIREMENT_CHECKERS registry (INV-12 compliant — engine uses boolean flags, not string comparisons), the field NAMES in the type interface are crime-justice vocabulary. An Economy module would need fields like 'hasTradeLicense' or 'isDebtor' — but the interface is defined in engine and can't be extended without engine changes. This partially breaks the Economy Module Test.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/agent-types.ts:101-106",
      "issue": "AgentProfile capability flags 'investigator', 'detersCrime', 'canSentence' are crime-justice-specific boolean capabilities baked into the engine type. An Economy module would need 'isMerchant', 'regulatesTrade', 'canAudit' — but these don't exist in the engine type. The engine would need type changes to support new capability domains. Should be a generic Map<string, boolean> or similar extensible structure.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/il.ts:594-690",
      "issue": "buildAccusationContext() is engine-level code that builds crime-specific accusation evaluation context. The entire accusation flow (evidence quality assessment, personality notes for investigators, 'I am the law here' response) is crime-justice logic in the engine. While the response handler is registry-based (registerAccusationResponseHandler), the evaluation logic and prompt building are inline.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "location": "packages/engine/src/journal.ts:1-48",
      "issue": "PlayerJournal with JournalEntry types ('clue' | 'testimony' | 'observation'), CaseSummary with suspect/perpetrator/evidence concepts — this is an investigation journal, a crime-justice module concept, implemented in the engine. An Economy module has no investigation journal. Should be in modules with a generic engine hook for player state tracking.",
      "severity": "minor"
    }
  ],
  "registryCoverage": "STRONG but NOT COMPLETE. Counting distinct registry data structures in event-registry.ts + al.ts + topic-engine.ts + evidence.ts + il.ts: EVENT_TYPE_FLAGS, SOURCE_FLAGS, EVENT_CATEGORIES, EVENT_LABELS, EVENT_GOAL_CLEANUP, EVENT_TO_SCENE_TYPE, DEATH_EVENTS, PURSUIT_EVENTS, EVENT_HANDLERS, ACTION_VERB_MAP, _secretEventType, CRIME_SCENE_TEMPLATES, RELATIONSHIP_LABELS, BELIEF_SOURCE_LABELS, SOURCE_TRANSFER_WEIGHTS, SOURCE_DECAY_RATES, STEP_HANDLERS, ACTIVE_STATUSES, ABSENT_STATUSES, INTERACT_POST_HOOKS, INTERACTION_RESULT_HOOKS, WITNESS_EVENT_TYPE, EVENT_MOOD_SHIFTS, GOAL_MOOD_BOOST, BODY_DISCOVERY_EVENT_TYPE, ROLE_TOPIC_POOLS, LOCATION_HOOKS, DISCOVERY_HANDLERS, RUMOR_EVENT_TYPE, BELIEF_CREATORS, MEMORY_TEXT_BUILDERS, DAY_ADVANCE_HANDLERS, SOURCE_CATEGORIES, TICK_PHASE_HANDLERS, SECRET_DIRECTIVE_BUILDERS, CONSEQUENCE_POST_HANDLERS, PRESSURE_TYPES, PRESSURE_HANDLERS, SPEECH_ACTS, CLAIM_SOURCES, IL_TRADE_VERB_SET, QUESTION_TOPICS, _defaultSpeechAct, ACCUSATION_RESPONSE_HANDLERS, AFFORDANCE_REGISTRY, TARGET_FINDERS, REACTIVE_TARGET_FINDERS, REQUIREMENT_CHECKERS, BLOCKING_GOAL_TYPES, EVIDENCE_RULES, EVIDENCE_SOURCES, TOPIC_SOURCE_REGISTRY, TOPIC_GROUP_MAP, LLM_TASKS = 53 registries. These comprehensively cover: event lifecycle (types, flags, labels, handlers, cleanup, mood shifts), belief system (sources, labels, weights, decay, creators), goal system (step handlers, mood boosts, blocking types), affordance system (registry, target finders, requirements, action verbs), consequence system (CG rules, post-handlers, memory text builders), discovery system (body/witness/crime-scene templates), conversation system (topic sources, groups, role pools, location hooks, interaction hooks), IL system (speech acts, claim sources, pressure types/handlers, question topics, trade verbs, accusation handlers), evidence system (rules, sources), and misc (day advance, tick phase, secret directives, status categories). GAPS: (1) AffordanceRequirements type fields are hardcoded, not registry-extensible; (2) AgentProfile capability flags are hardcoded; (3) buildRoleDirective prompt prose is hardcoded; (4) secret-directives behavioral prose is hardcoded; (5) 'rumor' source in world.ts conversation belief-transfer is hardcoded. The registries cover ~90% of the extension surface but leave 5 significant areas where engine code directly embeds crime-justice semantics.",
  "invariantStatus": "INV-14 (engine/module boundary): PARTIALLY VIOLATED. Zero import violations (engine never imports from modules/ — clean). But INV-14 says 'Zero module-specific string literals in engine code.' Engine contains: 'rumor' source string (world.ts:1181,1194,1309), 'body_discovery'/'deduction'/'evidence' in BELIEF_SOURCE constants (event-registry.ts:332-338), default labels and weights for these sources, crime-specific prose in prompts (agent.ts, secret-directives.ts, il.ts). The import boundary is perfect; the semantic boundary has leaks. INV-08 (event type flags): COMPLIANT. Engine checks isTransferable(), isPerpetrator(), isFirsthand(), isConcealment(), isPursuitEvent(), isDeathEvent() — all boolean flag functions. No event type string comparisons in engine logic. INV-09 (source flags): COMPLIANT for flag checks. Engine uses isPerpetrator(source), isFirsthand(source), isConcealment(source). However, engine also hardcodes 'rumor' as a source string, which is NOT checked via flags but used directly in addBelief calls. INV-10 (actionVerb registration): COMPLIANT. getAllRegisteredVerbs() used for schema enum. Module validates at boot (crime-justice/index.ts:554-558). INV-15 (no hardcoded fallback strings): MOSTLY COMPLIANT. getEventLabelSafe defaults to 'an incident' — generic, not domain-specific. getSourceTransferWeight defaults to 0.6 — numeric, acceptable. getSourceDecayRate defaults to 0.02 — numeric, acceptable. getRelationshipLabel falls back to type string itself — acceptable. getDefaultSpeechAct throws if not registered — correct fail-fast. One edge case: il.ts:607 passes 'wrongdoing' as fallback to getEventLabelSafe — slightly crime-flavored but arguably generic. INV-12 (socialOnly is boolean flag): COMPLIANT. Engine checks definition.socialOnly boolean, not affordance ID strings.",
  "notes": "OVERALL ASSESSMENT: The CLWA engine architecture is WELL-DESIGNED and MOSTLY delivers on its promise. The 53 registries represent genuine architectural commitment to module-agnosticism. For a new Economy module: (1) event types, CG rules, affordances, step handlers, target finders would all work through existing registries with zero engine changes — this is the STRONG 90%. (2) BUT: you would need engine changes for new AgentProfile capability flags (e.g. 'isMerchant'), new AffordanceRequirements fields (e.g. 'hasTradeGoods'), and you'd get crime-specific prompt prose leaking into NPC behavior. (3) The 'rumor' source hardcoded in world.ts is the most actionable bug — should be BELIEF_SOURCE.GOSSIP or a configurable string. (4) The pre-seeded defaults in event-registry.ts (BELIEF_SOURCE_LABELS, SOURCE_TRANSFER_WEIGHTS) are a minor smell — modules already override them, so they're technically dead defaults, but they document engine assumptions about what sources exist. (5) The biggest structural issue is AffordanceRequirements and AgentProfile — these are rigid engine types that must change for each new module domain. The fix: convert capability flags to a generic Map<string, boolean> and AffordanceRequirements to use only the REQUIREMENT_CHECKERS registry (which already exists and works!). The AffordanceRequirements type fields like 'isCriminal' should be removed from the type definition — they're already checked via registry. (6) The prompt prose in agent.ts/secret-directives.ts could be moved to a registry of 'directive builders' — secret-directives.ts already has this pattern (getSecretDirectiveBuilders), but the engine's own sections 1-3 should also be delegated to module-registered builders instead of being inline. PRIORITY: (A) Fix 'rumor' hardcode -> BELIEF_SOURCE constant or registry, (B) Make AffordanceRequirements fully registry-based (remove hardcoded field names), (C) Move prompt prose from agent.ts/secret-directives.ts inline sections to registered builders, (D) Clean up pre-seeded defaults. All are achievable without restructuring. The fundamental architecture pattern is CORRECT and PROVEN."
}

PROBE — TRADING ECONOMY:
{
  "genre": "Peaceful Trading Village — economy sim (produce/buy/sell/price/undercut/rivalry), zero crime/justice",
  "moduleOnlyNeeds": [
    "EVENT TYPES (TRADE_COMPLETED, PRICE_CHANGED, GOODS_PRODUCED, UNDERCUT): registerEventCategory + registerEventLabels + registerEventTypeFlags. Engine's EventType is just `string` (cg-types.ts:3); no union to extend.",
    "AFFORDANCES (haggle, produce, undercut, set_price, open_stall): push to AFFORDANCE_REGISTRY with OCEAN+H weights. The scoring engine (computeUtility in al.ts) is genre-agnostic — profit-greed maps cleanly to existing trait weights (low-A/low-H undercut, high-C produce) + moodWeight + relationshipWeight (business rivalry = negative sentiment). NO engine change for motivation.",
    "ACTION VERBS (sell, buy, haggle, undercut): registerActionVerbs() maps LLM verbs → affordance IDs. Volition schema enum auto-includes them (volition.ts:getVolitionSchema).",
    "STEP HANDLERS (produce_goods, execute_trade, change_price): registerStepHandler — engine runs them via the generic `default` case in goals.ts executeStep. Handlers mutate state, observe, adjust relationships, emit events exactly like steal_from/arrest do.",
    "CONSEQUENCE RULES (undercut → rival resentment, price spike → cascading distrust, monopoly → community anger): push ConsequenceRule[] into the CG rules array. cg-engine.ts is fully generic — conditions use trait/relationship/belief/has_capability/eq/gt/lt ops, targetMode witnesses/location/affected/all. Economic ripple = same machinery as crime cascade.",
    "BELIEFS about economic events (X overcharges, Y undercuts, Z hoards): beliefs.ts BeliefManager is event-type-agnostic — belief.eventType is a free string. registerBeliefCreator/registerMemoryTextBuilder/registerSourceDecayRates produce economic beliefs.",
    "TOPIC SOURCES (NPCs gossip about prices/deals): TOPIC_SOURCE_REGISTRY.push + registerTopicGroupMapping — same as social/index.ts.",
    "CAPABILITY FLAGS for roles (merchant/producer/guild-master): reuse the AgentProfile flag pattern; CG has_capability + REQUIREMENT_CHECKERS read arbitrary profile fields generically (cg-engine.ts:evaluateFilter reads profile[capFlag]).",
    "MOOD/SENTIMENT consequences of trade outcomes: registerEventMoodShifts, registerConsequencePostHandler, registerGoalMoodBoost — profit boosts mood, getting undercut drops it. Reuses SPEC-mood-modifiers."
  ],
  "forcedEngineChanges": [
    {
      "change": "Real economic STATE: wealth balance + inventory quantities + goods ownership on the agent. AgentState (agent-types.ts) has alive/status/location/mood/beliefs/relationships but NO numeric wealth, NO mutable inventory, NO ownership ledger. AgentProfile.shop is a STATIC ShopItem[] (id/name/price/description) with no quantity and no buy-side balance mutation.",
      "reason": "A trading-village core loop (accumulate wealth, deplete/replenish stock, transfer ownership on a sale) needs persistent mutable numeric state that survives save/load. A module can stash ad-hoc numbers in goal.data, but that is transient and not serialized as agent state; there is no engine-owned, persisted wealth/inventory field, no save.json schema slot, and no engine helper to atomically transfer value between two agents. This is genuinely a capability the engine lacks for ALL economic/RPG genres.",
      "classification": "new-universal-capability"
    },
    {
      "change": "Generalize the crime-specific AffordanceRequirement fields. al.ts AffordanceRequirement hardcodes isCriminal, isWanted, hasDetainedNpc, hasCrimeBelief, beliefConfidence, hasWitnessBelief, notPerpetrator, canSentence, hasSuspicionBelief, deductionBelowThreshold as named boolean fields on the ENGINE interface; checkRequirements/validatePlayerAction iterate REQUIREMENT_CHECKERS by these keys.",
      "reason": "A trade module needs requirement gates like hasStock, canAfford, ownsShop. The registry (REQUIREMENT_CHECKERS) is open, so a module CAN register new checker keys — but the typed AffordanceRequirement interface lists crime concepts by name, so a trade requirement is either untyped (cast) or forces adding economic fields to the engine type. Strictly a module CAN work via the generic record cast, but the engine TYPE leaks crime vocabulary; cleanly supporting trade requirements wants these moved to a generic shape.",
      "classification": "crime-assumption-leak"
    },
    {
      "change": "world.ts crime orchestration: markCrimeScene(), crimeScenes Map<string,CrimeScene[]>, onMurder callback, bodyDiscoveries set + body-discovery co-location loop, and the engine types CrimeScene/ExaminationPoint/CrimeSceneJSON (world-types.ts), TickResult.murder, TickResult.crimeScene.",
      "reason": "None of this fires in a no-crime village (no death events, no scene types registered), so it is inert rather than blocking. But it is dead crime-domain weight living in the engine: the engine OWNS the concept of a 'crime scene' and 'murder/body discovery' as first-class types and tick logic, instead of these being a module-registered 'world-marker' abstraction. A politics/economy world that wanted its OWN persistent location-marker concept (e.g. a 'market stall' or 'contested territory') would find the generic version absent — only the crime-named one exists.",
      "classification": "crime-assumption-leak"
    },
    {
      "change": "il.ts engine-resident TradeIntent: TradeIntent interface, tradeIntent JSON-schema branch, the hardcoded prompt line '\"trade\" = player wants to buy, sell, browse goods, or ask about prices', trade-detection correction logic, and EmotionalPressure.type union ('threat'|'bribe'|'flattery'|'guilt'|'intimidation').",
      "reason": "Interesting double-edge: the engine ALREADY contains primitive trade plumbing (player→NPC shop browse), so a trading village partly benefits for free. But it is a leak: trade concepts are baked into the engine Intent Layer rather than module-registered the way speech acts / pressure types / claim sources are. The EmotionalPressure type union is hardcoded (the runtime correctly uses getRegisteredPressureTypes(), but the TS type is fixed), so a module adding an economic pressure like 'lowball'/'price-gouge' gets runtime support yet a stale engine type. Module works around it, but extending trade semantics (negotiation, counteroffers, contracts) means editing engine il.ts.",
      "classification": "crime-assumption-leak"
    },
    {
      "change": "GoalActionResult.crimeEvent field (goals.ts) and TickResult.murder/crimeScene (world-types.ts) in the engine's public result shapes.",
      "reason": "A trade step handler that completes a sale has no 'crimeEvent' to populate; it would reuse the generic event path, but the engine result contract names a crime-specific channel. Purely a naming leak — does not block a module (the field is optional) — but proves crime vocabulary is in the engine's universal output types.",
      "classification": "crime-assumption-leak"
    }
  ],
  "verdict": "occasional-engine: the behavioral core (CG cascade, volition/PM scoring, goals/steps, beliefs, mood) is genuinely universal and a trading village is ~85% module-only — but it forces exactly ONE legitimate new-universal-capability (persistent mutable wealth/inventory/ownership state on the agent), and it surfaces several crime-assumption leaks (typed AffordanceRequirement fields, world.ts crime-scene orchestration, il.ts TradeIntent, GoalActionResult.crimeEvent) that a clean economy module would want cleaned up rather than worked-around.",
  "confidence": "high — claims are grounded in direct reads of al.ts, volition.ts, cg-engine.ts, cg-types.ts, goals.ts, agent-types.ts, world-types.ts, world.ts, il.ts, event-registry.ts and the crime-justice module (index/affordances/step-handlers). The one inference not 100% verified: that no wealth/inventory state exists anywhere — confirmed by a negative grep across packages (only comments/false-positives), but I did not read server.ts trade endpoint fully.",
  "notes": "Skeptical bottom line: the claim 'new game = new module, zero engine changes' is MOSTLY true for behavior/motivation/consequences but FALSE for state. The PM/volition pipeline cleanly supports profit/greed as a non-crime motive with no engine change — that part of the promise holds (computeUtility in al.ts is domain-agnostic; greed = low-H/low-A weights + relationshipWeight on rival sentiment). The CG engine generalizing to economic ripple effects also holds — cg-engine.ts has zero crime knowledge, everything is via getBeliefCreator/getMemoryTextBuilder registries. The genuine wall is STATE: there is no engine-owned, persisted, mutable wealth/inventory/ownership model and no atomic value-transfer helper — agent-types.ts AgentState lacks it and AgentProfile.shop is a static read-only price list. That is a real missing universal capability (every economy/RPG/4X world needs it), not a crime leak. Separately, the audit exposes that the engine is not crime-PURE either: it already leaks a primitive TradeIntent into il.ts — so the engine is milestone-coupled to BOTH crime and a half-built economy, which weakens INV-14's 'zero module-specific strings' claim. Recommendation if pursuing this genre: (1) add a generic numeric/resource state slab to AgentState + save schema (one-time universal investment), (2) refactor crime-named engine surfaces (AffordanceRequirement fields, CrimeScene→generic WorldMarker, TradeIntent→module-registered intent kind, crimeEvent→genericEvent) into neutral abstractions. After that one engine investment, subsequent economy content is genuinely module-only."
}

PROBE — COURT ROMANCE:
{
  "genre": "Royal Court Romance & Intrigue (deep-social, non-violent): courtship, romantic bonds, alliances, scheming for monarch's favor, flattering/damaging rumors; win = social standing / marriage.",
  "moduleOnlyNeeds": [
    "Event types COURTED/BOND_FORMED/FAVOR_GRANTED/RUMOR_SPREAD/BOND_BROKEN via registerEventCategory + registerEventLabels + registerEventTypeFlags (EventType is `string`, no engine union). RUMOR_SPREAD already exists as a registered crime event — proves it.",
    "Affordances court/flatter/scheme/betray-ally via AFFORDANCE_REGISTRY.push() — utility = OCEAN+H weights + moodWeight + relationshipWeight(sentimentCurve) + trustWeight + beliefModifiers; social/index.ts `socialize` proves non-crime affordances register cleanly.",
    "Custom multi-step social actions (propose_marriage, form_alliance, swear_fealty, denounce) via registerStepHandler + registerActionVerbs; engine resolveTemplates handles arbitrary module step actions generically.",
    "Rumor-as-influence: IL Claim->belief in applyDialogueMeaning is content-agnostic — gated only by isTransferable(eventType) registry flag + trust scaling -> TESTIMONY belief. NPC->NPC transferBeliefs + registerDayAdvanceHandler (the crime module's rumor spreader) are the exact hooks. registerBeliefCreator('RUMOR_SPREAD',...) already exists.",
    "Social ripples / consequence cascades: ConsequenceRule conditions already support op:'relationship' with selectable field (sentiment|trust), comparator, actor/toward, plus trait/belief/has_capability and targetMode witnesses|location|affected|all. Belief+memory+behavioral response fully delegated to registerBeliefCreator / registerMemoryTextBuilder / registerConsequencePostHandler.",
    "Motivation ambition/love: Enneagram coreFear/coreDesire (per-NPC data) + affordance trait weights drive PM utility; LLM has final say (volition). No engine change to model ambition (e.g. Type 3 courtier) or love (high-A toward target).",
    "Speech acts (court/flatter/propose), claim sources, question topics, relationship labels, pressure-type strings: all read from registries (getRegisteredSpeechActs/ClaimSources/QuestionTopics/PressureTypes, registerRelationshipLabels) and feed IL/volition JSON-schema enums at runtime.",
    "Capability flags for court roles: AgentProfile flags are read generically by op:'has_capability' in CG and by checkRequirements; a module convention (e.g. canGrantFavor) works as data — though see leak note, the *flag names* investigator/detersCrime/canSentence are typed on AgentProfile."
  ],
  "forcedEngineChanges": [
    {
      "change": "Add romantic bond + alliance relationship TYPES and a 'standing'/'favor' axis. relationships.ts hard-codes `RelationType = 'friend'|'rival'|'family'|'acquaintance'|'romantic'|'mentor'|'colleague'` as a TS union, with DEFAULT_TRUST/DEFAULT_SENTIMENT keyed by it and getGriefPenalty switch()ing on the literals. Relationship has only sentiment+trust — there is NO first-class social-standing/favor/rank scalar, which IS the genre's win condition.",
      "reason": "RelationType is a closed engine union, not a registry; 'alliance'/'betrothed'/'courting' need a new literal (engine edit) or string-loosening. There is no standing/favor field on Relationship, so 'scheme for the monarch's favor' (the core win metric) has no engine-native state to mutate — must be bolted on. Classification leans new-capability (a relationship graph that only tracks affect, not bond-type-as-data or standing, is a genuine universal gap), but the union being closed is also a typed-assumption leak.",
      "classification": "new-universal-capability"
    },
    {
      "change": "Make NPC role-directive prose registry-driven. agent.ts buildRoleDirective hardcodes crime-framed English: '## ACTIVE DUTY You are investigating the {crime} of {victim}. Suspect: {x}', 'Guilt gnaws at you', 'You witnessed something terrible', 'You are wanted for a crime'. Gated on registry flags (isTransferable/investigator/isPerpetrator/isFirsthand/isPursuitEvent) but the narrative interpretation is baked in.",
      "reason": "A courtier with a high-confidence transferable belief about a rivalry would render literal nonsense ('investigating the courtship of X. Suspect: Y'). No registry hook exists to supply alternative role-directive prose per belief-source-category; the engine owns the interpretation. Pure crime-justice assumption that leaked into the engine's prompt builder.",
      "classification": "crime-assumption-leak"
